1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Two brainy bicycle makers, a remote moonscape in North Carolina, and an impossible dream.

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Unlocking the secret of flight would take an inner tube box, a jewelry rig of hacksaw blades and bicycle spokes, and a two-wheeler with three rims.

3
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Explore the instruments and insight born of the brothers who had the right stuff.

4
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:32,000
stuck. Now, the technology of Kitty Hawk on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:52,100
It happened at a place so desolate, a visitor once likened himself to an Arctic explorer.

6
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:55,840
It lasted barely 12 seconds, just long enough for one

7
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:59,340
mesmerized witness behind a camera to snap the only

8
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:03,240
picture it would ever take.

9
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:07,940
On December 17, 1903, two high school dropouts from Dayton,

10
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:11,760
Ohio, taught the world how to fly.

11
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:15,560
That was then, and I think is still today,

12
00:01:15,660 --> 00:01:20,660
the symbol of our ability to fly, our ability to overcome

13
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:25,580
insurmountable problems. With that one photograph, now there was probably very

14
00:01:25,581 --> 00:01:30,320
little we weren't going to be able to do. If we could overcome the problem of

15
00:01:30,321 --> 00:01:33,780
flight, what else could we do?

16
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:46,840
A century after that eventful moment, we've mastered what was once the standard

17
00:01:46,841 --> 00:01:48,320
for human impossibility.

18
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,700
Every craft in our crowded stratosphere

19
00:01:56,701 --> 00:01:58,160
has one thing in common.

20
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:03,740
Each can trace its origins

21
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:05,760
to this revolutionary prototype.

22
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,000
The Wright Flyer,

23
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,260
enshrined in the Smithsonian Institution,

24
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:12,360
was the first aircraft conceived

25
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:14,540
with an understanding of the principles of flight.

26
00:02:14,541 --> 00:02:20,400
The principles applied to its design 100 years ago remain unchanged.

27
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,020
The two brothers who sent the century soaring

28
00:02:25,021 --> 00:02:28,120
were much more than tenacious bicycle mechanics.

29
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,260
They were consummate, self-taught experimenters

30
00:02:31,261 --> 00:02:35,520
who approached the problem of flight more methodically than any before them.

31
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:38,900
The Wright brothers were born problem solvers.

32
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:43,300
Their minds operated in such a way that they were very detailed,

33
00:02:43,301 --> 00:02:45,300
They were very focused.

34
00:02:45,301 --> 00:02:49,300
They worked with numbers and loved doing that.

35
00:02:49,301 --> 00:02:51,300
They were really the computer geeks of their day.

36
00:02:51,301 --> 00:02:57,300
These were two kids who had grown up in a household that appreciated technology,

37
00:02:57,301 --> 00:02:59,300
that appreciated mechanical skills.

38
00:02:59,301 --> 00:03:01,300
Their social life is very, very limited,

39
00:03:01,301 --> 00:03:05,300
and they seem to have thrown all their energy then into their work.

40
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:10,300
Wilbur and Orville's solemn work ethic came from their father, Milton,

41
00:03:10,301 --> 00:03:12,980
a bishop of the United Brethren Church.

42
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:18,540
They owed their mechanical talent to their mother, Susan, a born tinkerer.

43
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:23,400
As the boys grew up, their home in Dayton became a crucible of creativity.

44
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:29,220
With their parents' permission, they often skipped school to pursue their own interests.

45
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:33,400
Each developed a passion not for maidens, but machinery.

46
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,380
And both were destined to be lifelong bachelors.

47
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:42,500
By 1899, they were prosperous partners in the booming bicycle trade.

48
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,800
But seeking a supreme challenge,

49
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:50,320
they resolved to realize mankind's age-old dream of flight.

50
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,080
The Wright brothers began their quest to invent the airplane

51
00:03:54,081 --> 00:03:55,980
when the automobile was still a novelty.

52
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:02,100
In 1899, the gasoline engine was barely a decade old

53
00:04:02,101 --> 00:04:05,560
and only 8,000 cars rolled on America's roads.

54
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:10,180
less than 3% of Americans lived in homes powered by electricity.

55
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,080
Even fewer had a telephone.

56
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:18,560
It was a Victorian world, burgeoning with technological promise.

57
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:22,740
And the Wrights gained a new sense of purpose

58
00:04:22,741 --> 00:04:26,020
as they set out to unravel the greatest riddle of their time.

59
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:30,660
Countless others had tried and failed.

60
00:04:30,661 --> 00:04:35,740
Some efforts were comical.

61
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,620
Others were catastrophic.

62
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:44,760
But even as their dreams of flight came crashing back to Earth,

63
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:47,740
they gained a firm grasp of the challenge.

64
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:53,120
There was basically three things that needed to be solved to have a workable airplane.

65
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:55,440
You needed wings to create lift.

66
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:00,180
You needed an engine and propeller system to actually move the airplane forward

67
00:05:00,181 --> 00:05:01,800
so the wings could create lift.

68
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:05,520
And most importantly, you needed to be able to control the vehicle.

69
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,980
Control was flight's greatest unsolved mystery.

70
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:14,720
In Germany, renowned engineer Otto Lilienthal

71
00:05:14,721 --> 00:05:18,020
made over 2,000 glider flights in search of the answer.

72
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:22,860
He tried to remain stable or turn by shifting his weight.

73
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:29,040
His pioneering research ended abruptly during one flight in 1896

74
00:05:29,041 --> 00:05:32,240
when he plummeted 50 feet and was killed.

75
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,980
Accidents like Lilienthal's convinced many

76
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:39,480
that the only solution was to build a craft

77
00:05:39,481 --> 00:05:41,280
that was inherently stable in the air,

78
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:43,400
minimizing the need for a pilot.

79
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:48,080
The most prominent believer in this theory was Samuel Langley,

80
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,080
the revered head of the Smithsonian Institution.

81
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,500
In 1896, Langley launched the most successful

82
00:05:56,501 --> 00:05:58,260
miniature plane yet devised.

83
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:05,420
It was powered by a steam engine and stayed aloft for 90 seconds before landing softly in the Potomac River.

84
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:11,600
Langley envisioned a full-scale version that would be the first powered manned aircraft,

85
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:15,800
a plane so stable that a pilot would just be along for the ride.

86
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:20,560
As bicycle makers, the Wrights viewed the problem of control differently.

87
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:28,020
The Wright brothers thought that a bicycle was inherently unstable, but it could be controlled.

88
00:06:28,021 --> 00:06:30,020
It was a matter of balance.

89
00:06:30,021 --> 00:06:34,020
They realized how important balance is.

90
00:06:34,021 --> 00:06:38,020
And balance was the real secret

91
00:06:38,021 --> 00:06:40,020
when they started working on the airplane.

92
00:06:40,021 --> 00:06:43,020
Balancing a bicycle is very similar to balancing

93
00:06:43,021 --> 00:06:45,020
an early aircraft in flight,

94
00:06:45,021 --> 00:06:48,020
whereas the rider becomes one with the craft.

95
00:06:48,021 --> 00:06:51,020
In the case of the bicycle, a rider will continually adjust

96
00:06:51,021 --> 00:06:55,020
their weight from side to side, going into a turn,

97
00:06:55,021 --> 00:06:59,280
adjust their position of their hands and their arms and their legs.

98
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,820
And in a similar way, the Wrights approached the problem of flight,

99
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:07,540
understanding that they would have to make constant small adjustments to their aircraft.

100
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:12,760
The Wright brothers' challenge was to devise a control system

101
00:07:12,761 --> 00:07:14,780
a pilot could operate to make those adjustments.

102
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:19,640
Wilbur found the first piece of the puzzle hiding in plain sight,

103
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:23,040
where those who dreamed of flight had been looking for centuries.

104
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:31,680
Observing birds circling over Dayton, he felt certain they balanced and turned by twisting their wingtips.

105
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:38,060
Flexing one tip up and the other down, they created different degrees of lift for each wing.

106
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:43,980
This enabled them to keep their stability or bank to one side or the other.

107
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:47,440
Wilbur's observation was revolutionary.

108
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:52,100
But he still had to figure out how to mimic the trick in a flying machine.

109
00:07:52,101 --> 00:07:58,400
inspiration hit at the bicycle shop as he shot the breeze with a customer

110
00:07:58,401 --> 00:08:05,940
Wilbur was talking to him and he picked up an inner tube box and as he talked to the customer he

111
00:08:05,941 --> 00:08:13,480
began very absent-mindedly twisting the two ends of the box and suddenly he realized that what was

112
00:08:13,481 --> 00:08:19,320
happening with the box was the same thing that happened with the bird's wings and immediately

113
00:08:19,321 --> 00:08:20,693
he had an idea.

114
00:08:20,694 --> 00:08:27,740
He said, we should be able to replicate this in a wing on our machine to make

115
00:08:27,741 --> 00:08:33,479
it fly. The Wright brothers called their bold new concept wing warping.

116
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,580
They set out to test their

117
00:08:35,581 --> 00:08:40,909
theory by building a five-foot biplane kite out of bamboo and paper.

118
00:08:40,910 --> 00:08:43,260
It had cords that ran from the

119
00:08:43,261 --> 00:08:52,220
wings that crossed to sticks that Wilbur held, and as he moved those sticks, the wings warped,

120
00:08:52,221 --> 00:08:54,845
and he was able to bank the kite.

121
00:08:54,846 --> 00:08:59,300
He was also able, by moving the cords together, to cause

122
00:08:59,301 --> 00:09:03,500
the kite to dive and to climb.

123
00:09:03,501 --> 00:09:06,148
Up until that time, no one really had control over the kites.

124
00:09:06,149 --> 00:09:07,320
It was just at the mercy of

125
00:09:07,321 --> 00:09:12,620
the wind. But here he was, he was able to make it descend and climb on command. This

126
00:09:12,621 --> 00:09:19,060
had to be amazing because now you were able to control this kite in flight any way you wanted to.

127
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:24,780
The Wright's next challenge was to build a glider that a pilot could control just as adeptly.

128
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:31,100
Since it would have no engine, they needed a test site with strong winds to generate a steady flow

129
00:09:31,101 --> 00:09:33,078
of air around its wings.

130
00:09:33,079 --> 00:09:38,600
700 miles from Dayton, a remote village on the outer banks of North

131
00:09:38,601 --> 00:09:40,350
Carolina seemed ideal.

132
00:09:40,351 --> 00:09:45,920
Its sands promised soft landings, and its solitude ensured the secrecy

133
00:09:45,921 --> 00:09:48,286
the guarded rights demanded.

134
00:09:48,287 --> 00:09:53,780
Kitty Hawk would become a turn-of-the-century version of Area 51.

135
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:58,602
September 1900.

136
00:09:58,603 --> 00:10:05,100
The Wright brothers' quest for flight began in a place so remote that no one

137
00:10:05,101 --> 00:10:08,637
in the closest town could tell them how to get there.

138
00:10:08,638 --> 00:10:11,040
Kitty Hawk was as obscure as the two

139
00:10:11,041 --> 00:10:14,216
bicycle makers who hoped to make history there.

140
00:10:14,217 --> 00:10:17,460
This was a wild, chaotic area at the turn of the

141
00:10:17,461 --> 00:10:18,161
century.

142
00:10:18,162 --> 00:10:24,400
Of course, this area behind us, we see a lot of grass and trees today, but in 1902 and

143
00:10:24,401 --> 00:10:29,819
1903, it was devoid of grass and trees. Orville described this area as a Sahara.

144
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:30,700
It was just a

145
00:10:30,701 --> 00:10:36,240
large dune field with four large sand dunes without a building or a tree, nothing that

146
00:10:36,241 --> 00:10:38,320
would impede the Wright Brothers in their flights.

147
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:44,520
The Wrights hoped to take advantage of Kitty Hawk's constant winds aboard a larger version

148
00:10:44,521 --> 00:10:46,140
of their 1899 kite.

149
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:51,600
Their glider weighed 52 pounds and spanned 17 feet.

150
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:57,780
The brothers crafted the wing ribs from ash and covered them with French sateen, a cotton

151
00:10:57,781 --> 00:10:59,320
fabric with a satin finish.

152
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:04,700
Relying on the late Otto Lilienthal's research on lift and wing shapes,

153
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:08,200
they designed a gentle curvature into the wing's upper surfaces.

154
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:12,800
The glider's control system featured two elements.

155
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:16,860
A forward-mounted elevator enabled the pilot to nose up or down,

156
00:11:17,140 --> 00:11:21,800
and the innovative wing warping system, which was linked to a foot-operated T-bar.

157
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:27,340
In this fashion, by pushing with your feet, you could pull on the cables that would warp the wings.

158
00:11:27,341 --> 00:11:32,520
If you pushed back with your right foot, then that would warp the wings to help you turn to the right.

159
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:36,260
And if you pushed with your left foot, then that would help you turn to the left.

160
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:41,180
The rights began by flying the glider as a tethered kite,

161
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:44,380
with lines running to the ground to operate the controls.

162
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:49,280
The elevator worked pretty good. The wings worked relatively well.

163
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:56,740
But overall, they were kind of dismayed because it didn't seem to generate as much lift as it calculated out to be.

164
00:11:56,741 --> 00:12:01,840
They used all the tables of the time based upon all the engineering knowledge at the time,

165
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:06,680
and the thing just didn't generate as much lift as it should have.

166
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:12,560
So that year, they were unable to really fly that glider as a true manned glider.

167
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:16,980
At one point, they did enlist one of the local children, Tom Tate was his name,

168
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:20,960
to get on the glider, and they flew it as a kite with him on board.

169
00:12:20,961 --> 00:12:27,600
And with him on board, they could now calculate the resistance of a seated body in their craft.

170
00:12:27,940 --> 00:12:33,600
So they did learn something while they were out here, but the glider was really not very successful.

171
00:12:35,340 --> 00:12:42,260
After six weeks, Wilbur and Orville returned to Dayton to tend to their bicycle business and to design a new glider.

172
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:48,420
They were confident that if they increased the size of the wings, it would produce the necessary lift.

173
00:12:48,421 --> 00:12:52,700
In the nation's capital, Samuel Langley was just as confident.

174
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:58,520
His winged masterwork, called the Great Aerodrome, was taking shape.

175
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:05,540
With engineer Charles Manley, he was busy designing a powerful engine he was certain held the key to its success.

176
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:11,220
Undaunted by Langley's progress, the Wrights returned to Kitty Hawk in 1901.

177
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:17,260
They assembled a new, larger glider in a hangar they erected themselves.

178
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:22,140
Its wings were 22 feet long and 7 feet wide,

179
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:25,360
with the surface area almost double their original model.

180
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:28,560
As they had done the year before,

181
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:31,720
the Wrights began by flying the glider as a kite.

182
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:34,880
Unlike any inventors before them,

183
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,440
they chronicled every step of their progress,

184
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,700
or lack of it, with photographs.

185
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,220
The Wright brothers were fine, amateur photographers,

186
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:47,560
and we're fortunate that they were

187
00:13:47,561 --> 00:13:49,800
because they documented everything that they did.

188
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,180
They were afraid that people weren't going to believe them,

189
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:56,380
and this was a way that they could have proof on film

190
00:13:56,381 --> 00:13:57,500
of what they were doing.

191
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:01,520
On July 27, 1901,

192
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:05,120
Wilbur piloted the new glider on its maiden test flights.

193
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:08,860
Kitty Hawk's locals were impressed,

194
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:11,940
but the rights were discouraged.

195
00:14:12,740 --> 00:14:14,140
Despite the increased wing area,

196
00:14:14,141 --> 00:14:15,980
there was no improvement in lift,

197
00:14:15,981 --> 00:14:18,840
And that wasn't their only setback.

198
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:24,400
It did not seem to fly as well at all as the 1900 glider.

199
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:28,020
Quite frankly speaking, the 1901 glider was a flying pig.

200
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:32,480
It was a terrible flying machine compared to their anticipated performance.

201
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:36,280
They suffered terrible problems with the control system.

202
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:41,820
The control system doesn't work anywhere near as well as it had in 1900,

203
00:14:41,821 --> 00:14:47,220
particularly in terms of pitch, controlling the glider up and down.

204
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:50,620
Turning the glider proved even more problematic.

205
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:55,400
They found on occasion that as they tried to roll the vehicle

206
00:14:55,401 --> 00:14:59,420
or maneuver it in a certain direction, for example, making a right turn,

207
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:04,020
all of a sudden they would find instead that the glider started to turn

208
00:15:04,021 --> 00:15:05,940
completely in the opposite direction.

209
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:07,540
And this was a great puzzlement.

210
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:10,840
Here I am in a right-hand turn, and all of a sudden the thing's going back to the left.

211
00:15:10,841 --> 00:15:13,380
why is this happening? They really weren't sure.

212
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:18,360
Wilbur's piloting skills made up for the glider's design flaws.

213
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:22,240
He was able to stay airborne as long as 18 seconds,

214
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:24,620
soaring as far as 400 feet.

215
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:27,160
It was little consolation.

216
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:30,940
They had broken almost every gliding record in the world,

217
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:34,320
yet they considered that whole year to be a failure.

218
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:36,140
They didn't have the lift they needed,

219
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:38,520
and they didn't have the control that they needed.

220
00:15:38,521 --> 00:15:46,020
On the train ride back to Dayton at the end of that season in 1901, Wilbur turned to Orville and said,

221
00:15:46,220 --> 00:15:49,120
not within a thousand years will man fly.

222
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:57,360
That was probably a really tough time for them to decide whether they should start all over and keep on going

223
00:15:57,361 --> 00:15:59,620
or whether they should just abandon this.

224
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:04,180
Most others before them had folded up their wings and gone home.

225
00:16:04,181 --> 00:16:10,700
But the Tenacious Brothers would use a flimsy assemblage of bicycle spokes and hacksaw blades

226
00:16:10,701 --> 00:16:14,360
to help them go where no man had gone before.

227
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:19,500
September, 1901.

228
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:23,400
Two brothers named Wright never felt more wrong.

229
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:29,360
Three years of flight experimentation had seemingly reached a dead end.

230
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:31,140
The Wright Brothers weren't quitters.

231
00:16:31,141 --> 00:16:33,420
It just wasn't in their genes.

232
00:16:34,180 --> 00:16:39,080
And they were doggedly determined to solve this problem.

233
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:43,780
To go forward, the Wright brothers realized they really had to take a step back.

234
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:49,100
Wilbur and Orville retraced their steps over the past three years

235
00:16:49,101 --> 00:16:52,060
and suspected where they had made their critical mistake.

236
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:55,320
From the beginning, they had relied on the lift data

237
00:16:55,321 --> 00:16:58,740
compiled by the late great German engineer Otto Lilienthal.

238
00:17:00,380 --> 00:17:03,880
His tables had been the key to how they designed the size and shape

239
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:04,940
of their glider's wings.

240
00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:09,180
The Wrights now believed what other aeronautic pioneers

241
00:17:09,181 --> 00:17:10,520
would consider sacrilege,

242
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:14,220
that Lilienthal's data was seriously flawed.

243
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:18,620
To test their theory, the Innovative Brothers

244
00:17:18,621 --> 00:17:21,440
had to look no further than the inventory in their shop.

245
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:25,180
The Wrights took a bicycle, and on the top,

246
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:29,440
they mounted upon the handlebars a free rotating wheel.

247
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:33,280
And on this wheel, the Wrights mounted a flat plate,

248
00:17:33,281 --> 00:17:35,040
and a curved airfoil.

249
00:17:35,860 --> 00:17:38,300
And their idea was that if they rode into a strong headwind,

250
00:17:38,540 --> 00:17:41,020
that wheel with those foils on top of it

251
00:17:41,021 --> 00:17:43,680
should remain stationary in a strong wind,

252
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:46,080
if Lilienthal's data was correct.

253
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:51,540
However, the wheel on the handlebars did not remain stationary,

254
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:54,140
and so they determined that, indeed,

255
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:56,560
there was a problem somewhere in the tables of data.

256
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,040
To find out for themselves what kind of wing

257
00:18:02,041 --> 00:18:03,220
produced the greatest lift,

258
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:08,820
The Wrights built what others had conceived 30 years earlier, a wind tunnel.

259
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:13,880
They crafted it from a wooden box that held laundry starch.

260
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:21,060
Power was supplied by a one-cylinder gas engine used to run the bicycle shop's machine tools.

261
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:26,180
It drove a fan that created a 23-mile-per-hour wind stream.

262
00:18:27,220 --> 00:18:31,080
Now, you'll notice as you look into the wind tunnel, you've got some straighteners in here.

263
00:18:31,081 --> 00:18:34,820
Once you get past this X straightener, you have sort of a honeycomb.

264
00:18:35,260 --> 00:18:40,040
This would straighten the wind and give it a nice smooth flow all the way through.

265
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:43,800
Now, at the forend of the wind tunnel, the Wrights put a balance.

266
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:46,980
That balance is how they measured lift.

267
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:50,360
This balance was a very simple and crude affair.

268
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:56,780
It was made of old bicycle spokes and hacksaw blades, but they produced very accurate data.

269
00:18:56,781 --> 00:19:03,980
For each wind tunnel test, the Wrights vertically mounted a miniature wing, or airfoil, on the balance.

270
00:19:05,920 --> 00:19:08,920
Each airfoil had its own unique curvature and shape.

271
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:16,480
Mounted near the airfoil was a series of flat plates with known values of drag.

272
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:24,380
By carefully measuring the difference between the airfoil's effect on the airflow and that of the flat plates,

273
00:19:24,381 --> 00:19:27,240
the brothers could calculate the airfoil's lift.

274
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:32,460
Over three months, they tested nearly 200 different airfoils.

275
00:19:34,460 --> 00:19:38,260
The Wrights, like all flight pioneers, knew that a wing produced lift

276
00:19:38,261 --> 00:19:42,740
by creating reduced air pressure above and increased pressure below.

277
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,320
Their challenge was to discover which shape would create the greatest degree of lift.

278
00:19:49,240 --> 00:19:53,740
Up until that time, people knew that curved wings were much more efficient

279
00:19:53,741 --> 00:19:56,360
at generating lift than a flat plate was.

280
00:19:56,560 --> 00:19:59,380
The problem was is they were generally pretty symmetrical.

281
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:00,800
The high point was right in the center.

282
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:04,140
What they found was is that you really want to have the highest part

283
00:20:04,141 --> 00:20:07,960
about a third of the way back, not halfway or not way up front

284
00:20:07,961 --> 00:20:11,000
like they did on their 1901 and 1900 gliders.

285
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:14,400
Armed with their breakthrough findings,

286
00:20:14,401 --> 00:20:17,480
the Wrights returned to Kitty Hawk in August 1902.

287
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:23,520
They cannibalized their 1901 glider and crafted a new model,

288
00:20:23,740 --> 00:20:30,180
They not only modified the curvature of the airfoil, but made the wings 10 feet longer

289
00:20:30,181 --> 00:20:32,220
and 2 feet narrower.

290
00:20:32,221 --> 00:20:37,900
They also added a fixed vertical tail, reasoning that it would add to the glider's stability.

291
00:20:37,901 --> 00:20:41,620
Finally, the brothers re-examined the glider's control system.

292
00:20:41,621 --> 00:20:48,380
In the 1902 glider, the elevator worked the same way it did in the 1900 and 1901 glider.

293
00:20:48,381 --> 00:20:53,760
You had a bar that you held in your hand, and that moved the elevator up and down this

294
00:20:53,761 --> 00:20:55,100
way.

295
00:20:55,101 --> 00:21:00,080
They did make a fairly significant change in the wing warping in 1902.

296
00:21:00,081 --> 00:21:05,940
In 1900 and 1901, the wings had been warped by moving a foot bar back and forth, and the

297
00:21:05,941 --> 00:21:10,940
Wright brothers found this to be a very difficult and frustrating way to move the wings.

298
00:21:10,941 --> 00:21:15,460
So in 1902, they changed and went to the hip cradle.

299
00:21:15,461 --> 00:21:24,420
Now when they wanted to warp the wings, they moved back and forth this way.

300
00:21:24,421 --> 00:21:30,660
They found this to be much simpler and it gave them better overall control of the glider.

301
00:21:30,661 --> 00:21:34,920
During their first free glides, the Wrights discovered that the new craft's wings generated

302
00:21:34,921 --> 00:21:39,240
precisely the lift they had calculated.

303
00:21:39,241 --> 00:21:43,940
The new tail seemed to solve the problem of the glider reversing course during a turn,

304
00:21:43,941 --> 00:21:45,380
a vexing new problem arose.

305
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:48,880
At times, the glider slipped sideways,

306
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:52,740
tipped on its low wing, and spun into the sand.

307
00:21:53,880 --> 00:21:55,800
Every flight seemed fraught with risk.

308
00:21:56,500 --> 00:22:00,880
In September 1902, the Wrights faced their closest brush with disaster.

309
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:07,460
Orville was flying along, and the glider started to go out of control.

310
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:10,500
It was pitching upwards, it was rolling to the side,

311
00:22:10,501 --> 00:22:13,800
and then it was turning about its own vertical axis.

312
00:22:13,940 --> 00:22:15,900
In brief, it went completely out of control.

313
00:22:19,320 --> 00:22:21,460
Orville stands up out of the wreckage,

314
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:23,620
climbs out of it, brushes himself off.

315
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:25,280
He is totally unscratched.

316
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:27,960
He only has one small tear in his clothing.

317
00:22:28,560 --> 00:22:30,460
So he had a miraculous escape.

318
00:22:31,400 --> 00:22:34,080
But this did really drive home the point to him

319
00:22:34,081 --> 00:22:37,480
there is something inherently wrong with this glider.

320
00:22:38,600 --> 00:22:40,100
At the Wright brothers' camp,

321
00:22:40,101 --> 00:22:43,140
it was time for their unique approach to problem solving.

322
00:22:43,940 --> 00:22:50,400
They had a good time arguing about these problems that they were trying to solve with flight.

323
00:22:50,401 --> 00:22:54,140
They would argue so vehemently that they would be shouting at one another.

324
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:58,800
One would take one side of a problem and the other would take the other side of the problem

325
00:22:58,801 --> 00:23:04,400
and they would argue and argue and then finally at the end they would realize they had switched sides

326
00:23:04,401 --> 00:23:09,100
and one would be arguing the side that the other had argued previously.

327
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:12,280
After a while they would generally come to an agreement.

328
00:23:12,281 --> 00:23:19,040
But by doing this, they would really look at every angle, every side of an issue

329
00:23:19,041 --> 00:23:24,500
to determine what was truly valuable, necessary, and accurate information.

330
00:23:26,020 --> 00:23:29,660
The unusual procedure yielded two inspired suggestions.

331
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:35,580
Orville proposed that they hinge the fixed vertical tail of their glider to make it movable.

332
00:23:36,940 --> 00:23:38,140
Wilbur added a new wrinkle.

333
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:41,940
He suggested they link it with pulleys to the wing warping system.

334
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:48,060
With one shift of the hips, the pilot could twist the wings and turn the tail simultaneously.

335
00:23:50,300 --> 00:23:55,240
On October 10, 1902, the Wrights were ready to test their revamped glider.

336
00:23:57,060 --> 00:24:03,020
Four years of tenacious calculation and painstaking work had boiled down to one critical moment.

337
00:24:05,860 --> 00:24:07,100
Wilbur manned the controls.

338
00:24:07,101 --> 00:24:12,840
Orville and a Kitty Hawk local aimed the glider directly into the stiff wind.

339
00:24:25,080 --> 00:24:31,940
Wilbur throws his hips over to induce a roll to the right for a coordinated turn.

340
00:24:31,941 --> 00:24:40,540
And for the first time in history, we have true flight, ability to turn and completely control a craft in the air.

341
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:53,500
The Wrights flew the revolutionary craft on 375 flights that October, the best lasting 26 seconds.

342
00:24:54,120 --> 00:24:56,248
They were able to fly in any wind.

343
00:24:56,249 --> 00:24:59,940
They were able to stay in the air for long periods of time.

344
00:24:59,941 --> 00:25:03,080
They were able to fly over 600 feet on some of those glides,

345
00:25:03,320 --> 00:25:04,940
and they knew they had control of the wind.

346
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:08,580
They had conquered the air in October of 1902.

347
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:12,900
Wilbur and Orville had no time to celebrate.

348
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:16,820
Their most formidable rival, Samuel Langley,

349
00:25:16,980 --> 00:25:19,020
was nearing completion of his great aerodrome.

350
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:23,360
He was determined to have a pilot fly it into history.

351
00:25:25,080 --> 00:25:27,520
The underdog Wrights had more to worry about

352
00:25:27,521 --> 00:25:29,420
than converting their glider into an airplane.

353
00:25:29,421 --> 00:25:33,140
they also had to keep a close eye on the clock.

354
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:40,320
In 1903, the quest for flight became a great race.

355
00:25:41,800 --> 00:25:45,340
As the Wright brothers toiled in anonymity in their Dayton, Ohio workshop,

356
00:25:46,160 --> 00:25:49,200
Samuel Langley was making headlines as the overwhelming favorite.

357
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:52,940
With the help of his pilot, Charles Manley,

358
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:57,700
the Smithsonian's eminent leader was gearing up for the maiden flight of his great aerodrome.

359
00:25:57,701 --> 00:26:01,620
It was powered by a 52-horsepower engine

360
00:26:01,621 --> 00:26:05,260
and sported tandem wings spanning 48 feet.

361
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:08,360
Langley's confidence was supreme.

362
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:12,080
So for all that the Wrights knew, Langley might beat them.

363
00:26:12,220 --> 00:26:14,460
They were well aware that he had a very good chance to beat them.

364
00:26:14,900 --> 00:26:18,160
They certainly had great respect for his own talents, his own capabilities,

365
00:26:18,480 --> 00:26:21,360
but the Wrights were going ahead in their own good way in time.

366
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:23,700
They certainly weren't going to try to fly prematurely

367
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:26,280
just for the sake of trying to win a race.

368
00:26:26,281 --> 00:26:28,400
they were going to fly when they were good and ready.

369
00:26:29,420 --> 00:26:33,740
Even so, Wilbur and Orville pressed forward with a new sense of urgency.

370
00:26:35,180 --> 00:26:37,760
Although they had solved the riddle of aircraft control,

371
00:26:38,360 --> 00:26:40,700
they had to unravel a whole new aeronautic puzzle.

372
00:26:42,520 --> 00:26:44,720
They needed a light yet powerful engine,

373
00:26:45,740 --> 00:26:48,060
effective propellers to produce adequate thrust,

374
00:26:48,061 --> 00:26:52,540
and a sturdy new frame to carry the weight and withstand the vibration.

375
00:26:52,541 --> 00:26:57,500
The craft the Wrights would call the Whopper flying machine

376
00:26:57,501 --> 00:27:01,940
would be a fusion of innovative breakthroughs made with unparalleled speed.

377
00:27:03,560 --> 00:27:05,440
Wilbur and Orville crafted their own engine

378
00:27:05,441 --> 00:27:08,680
with the help of their chief bicycle mechanic, Charlie Taylor.

379
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:12,220
They cast the crankcase from aluminum,

380
00:27:12,860 --> 00:27:15,700
which had only become commercially available in the past decade.

381
00:27:16,820 --> 00:27:20,840
The rest of the engine was primitive, even by turn-of-the-century standards.

382
00:27:21,580 --> 00:27:24,200
It had no fuel pump, carburetor, or spark plugs.

383
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:30,120
Still, it produced 12 horsepower and weighed only 179 pounds.

384
00:27:31,680 --> 00:27:37,100
As Taylor made the engine, the Wrights struggled with an even greater challenge, the propellers.

385
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:43,660
To that time, flight pioneers had assumed that an aircraft propeller would function much like a ship's,

386
00:27:44,700 --> 00:27:48,420
pushing air instead of water backward to drive a plane forward.

387
00:27:48,421 --> 00:27:53,420
Months of study convinced Wilbur otherwise.

388
00:27:53,421 --> 00:28:01,420
Wilbur's idea that was very different was that a propeller should be a wing on its side,

389
00:28:01,421 --> 00:28:09,420
traveling in a spiral course and producing lift horizontally or producing thrust.

390
00:28:09,421 --> 00:28:14,420
Applying the revolutionary theory, the Wrights carved two spruce propellers,

391
00:28:14,421 --> 00:28:17,420
each eight and a half feet in diameter.

392
00:28:17,421 --> 00:28:22,420
The Wrights used two propellers for a very specific reason.

393
00:28:22,421 --> 00:28:27,620
They had determined that two propellers turning at a lower RPM would act on a greater volume

394
00:28:27,621 --> 00:28:33,320
of air and give them the thrust that they required a lot easier, better and more efficiently

395
00:28:33,321 --> 00:28:36,980
than one propeller turning at a higher RPM.

396
00:28:36,981 --> 00:28:42,020
They could get by with a less powerful, lighter engine with the two propellers.

397
00:28:42,021 --> 00:28:47,120
Fittingly, the Wrights linked their propellers to their engine with a bicycle chain

398
00:28:47,121 --> 00:28:47,925
transmission.

399
00:28:47,926 --> 00:28:52,820
They twisted one of the drive chains into a figure eight to make the propellers

400
00:28:52,821 --> 00:28:55,071
spin in opposite directions.

401
00:28:55,072 --> 00:29:00,780
The counter rotation was a design wrinkle born of their gifted insight.

402
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:08,779
A propeller spinning produces torque, which is a sideways pull on the airframe.

403
00:29:08,780 --> 00:29:09,960
If the Wright

404
00:29:09,961 --> 00:29:16,620
Brothers propellers had spun in the same direction, then the torque would have tended to turn the

405
00:29:16,621 --> 00:29:19,031
airplane to the right or to the left.

406
00:29:19,032 --> 00:29:22,940
And so they had the propeller spin in opposite directions to

407
00:29:22,941 --> 00:29:24,208
cancel out that torque.

408
00:29:24,209 --> 00:29:28,620
It's fascinating to me that this is something that they thought of at that time.

409
00:29:30,220 --> 00:29:34,498
Compared to their gliders, the right flyer was massive.

410
00:29:34,499 --> 00:29:37,220
The 1902 glider weighed 120 pounds.

411
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:39,609
The flyer weighed 605.

412
00:29:39,610 --> 00:29:45,660
The engine, comprising nearly a third of that weight, was mounted to the

413
00:29:45,661 --> 00:29:46,921
right of the pilot.

414
00:29:46,922 --> 00:29:51,900
Since it outweighed each of the brothers by 34 pounds, it made the aircraft

415
00:29:51,901 --> 00:29:52,633
unbalanced.

416
00:29:52,634 --> 00:29:58,500
To compensate, they added four inches to the wing on the heavy side to create more lift.

417
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:06,460
The controls remained unchanged, except for yet another refinement to the wing warping mechanism.

418
00:30:07,200 --> 00:30:13,640
In the 1902 glider, the entire wing, both the leading edge and the trailing edge, would flex

419
00:30:13,641 --> 00:30:17,264
when they activated the wing warping system.

420
00:30:17,265 --> 00:30:20,640
Now on this 1903 flyer, this leading edge

421
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:25,960
was solid. It did not move. It was fixed. The back portion of it, however, the back

422
00:30:25,961 --> 00:30:31,660
of the wings, both upper and lower, would flex. Now as those were connected into the

423
00:30:31,661 --> 00:30:41,660
hip cradle, you can see what happens as the wing is warped. With the twin

424
00:30:43,640 --> 00:30:48,520
The twisting motion in the back part of these wings, as it was on the 1903 flyer,

425
00:30:48,521 --> 00:30:53,640
it's really much the same as what we see on modern aircraft with an aileron.

426
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:59,060
In October 1903, Wilbur and Orville returned to Kitty Hawk.

427
00:31:00,400 --> 00:31:05,040
It took several weeks to assemble the flyer and ready it for its critical first flight.

428
00:31:06,640 --> 00:31:08,580
They feared they were too late.

429
00:31:09,520 --> 00:31:14,800
On December 8th, Langley invited the press to witness the launch of his great aerodrome

430
00:31:14,801 --> 00:31:16,800
from a houseboat on the Potomac River.

431
00:31:18,880 --> 00:31:24,200
When the aerodrome was catapulted from its launch rail, its rear wings collapsed.

432
00:31:24,820 --> 00:31:26,520
The plane plummeted in pieces.

433
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:33,220
The pilot escaped unhurt, but the disaster made Langley a laughingstock.

434
00:31:33,740 --> 00:31:36,460
It was an extraordinary moment.

435
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:42,200
If this scientist, the man of international renown, could not successfully develop a flying machine,

436
00:31:42,820 --> 00:31:44,960
then the likelihood was no one could.

437
00:31:46,780 --> 00:31:52,640
Nine days after Langley's failure, the Wright brothers prepared to fly in the face of conventional wisdom.

438
00:31:54,280 --> 00:32:00,620
Since the flyer had no wheels, they built a 60-foot launch rail out of 2x4s laid end to end.

439
00:32:01,560 --> 00:32:04,380
The aircraft's skids rested on a small dolly.

440
00:32:05,380 --> 00:32:08,980
The Wrights recruited several members of Kitty Hawk's life-saving team

441
00:32:08,981 --> 00:32:10,600
to act as their ground crew.

442
00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:14,260
They instructed one of them, John Daniels,

443
00:32:14,380 --> 00:32:15,900
to stand ready with their camera.

444
00:32:17,340 --> 00:32:21,500
A toss of a coin determined that Orville would take the controls.

445
00:32:22,060 --> 00:32:24,060
The brothers kind of went off to one side,

446
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:27,380
spoke to each other quietly, and shook each other's hand.

447
00:32:27,381 --> 00:32:31,840
And there was a comment by one of the life-savers that day,

448
00:32:31,841 --> 00:32:34,860
and he said, it looked like they were almost saying goodbye,

449
00:32:35,900 --> 00:32:37,820
like they would never see each other again.

450
00:32:39,500 --> 00:32:43,520
At 10.35 a.m., Orville boarded the flyer,

451
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:45,100
and the engine was started.

452
00:32:45,560 --> 00:32:46,760
The noise was definite.

453
00:32:47,220 --> 00:32:48,560
They can't hear each other talk.

454
00:32:48,620 --> 00:32:51,220
They can only communicate by looking at one another and nodding.

455
00:32:51,580 --> 00:32:52,680
Orville looks over at Wilbur.

456
00:32:52,681 --> 00:32:54,040
Wilbur returns the look.

457
00:32:54,300 --> 00:32:55,320
Orville gives the nod.

458
00:32:55,540 --> 00:32:57,520
He releases that whole back wire,

459
00:32:57,900 --> 00:32:59,640
and that craft starts down the rail.

460
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:08,300
The flyer rose gracefully, then followed a meandering up and down course as Orville struggled with the elevator.

461
00:33:09,260 --> 00:33:12,320
After 120 feet, it darted into the sand.

462
00:33:13,140 --> 00:33:18,520
John Daniels was so excited about seeing the aircraft lift off the rail

463
00:33:18,521 --> 00:33:22,800
that he wasn't even certain if he had squeezed the shutter bulb to take the photograph.

464
00:33:24,300 --> 00:33:25,700
Daniels needn't have worried.

465
00:33:27,800 --> 00:33:30,260
Initially, it doesn't look like there's a lot there.

466
00:33:30,261 --> 00:33:34,120
But if you really look at that, it's much more detailed than you think.

467
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:36,100
You can see the launching rail.

468
00:33:36,420 --> 00:33:38,640
You can see the little dolly that the plane was on.

469
00:33:39,240 --> 00:33:43,640
You can see the tools they used that day, a shovel to level the ground.

470
00:33:43,820 --> 00:33:47,940
You can see a little grease bucket that they used to grease the rail that the plane ran along,

471
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:50,580
a dry cell battery to start the engine.

472
00:33:51,780 --> 00:33:56,440
A professional photographer could not have done it any better than John Daniels did that day.

473
00:33:56,441 --> 00:34:00,860
And as far as I can determine, that's the only photograph that John Daniels ever took.

474
00:34:02,300 --> 00:34:08,620
An hour after their first flight, Wilbur piloted the second, covering 175 feet.

475
00:34:10,320 --> 00:34:12,080
Orville flew the third for 200.

476
00:34:13,380 --> 00:34:22,420
Then, on flight number four that December day, Wilbur kept the flyer aloft 59 seconds, soaring 852 feet.

477
00:34:22,421 --> 00:34:29,400
Minutes later, a gust of wind overturned the flyer and sent it hurtling across the sand.

478
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:32,360
The damage was beyond what they could quickly repair.

479
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:34,420
It would never fly again.

480
00:34:36,160 --> 00:34:41,240
The Wright's unique talents had made flight, humankind's impossible dream, a reality.

481
00:34:42,400 --> 00:34:46,340
But their mistrust of others would nearly cost them the credit for it.

482
00:34:46,341 --> 00:34:54,860
The Wrights' triumph at Kitty Hawk got the young century off to a flying start.

483
00:34:56,380 --> 00:34:58,480
But the world was slow to take notice.

484
00:34:59,660 --> 00:35:05,020
Only four newspapers carried the story the following day, which most people dismissed

485
00:35:05,021 --> 00:35:05,760
as exaggerated.

486
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:13,220
The Wrights fueled doubts by refusing to release the photo of their first flight, fearing that

487
00:35:13,221 --> 00:35:15,060
competitors would pirate their designs.

488
00:35:16,340 --> 00:35:22,100
Wilbur and Orville pressed on, focused more on making improvements than headlines.

489
00:35:23,060 --> 00:35:26,580
Though revolutionary, their flyer was an unstable aircraft.

490
00:35:27,140 --> 00:35:28,240
Only they could have flown.

491
00:35:28,241 --> 00:35:31,880
It still had problems with pitch.

492
00:35:32,060 --> 00:35:35,240
By pitch, I mean the up and down controls.

493
00:35:35,820 --> 00:35:38,560
Every single flight that day, all four of them,

494
00:35:38,980 --> 00:35:43,520
ended with a dart toward the ground that they couldn't control.

495
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:45,500
It was difficult to control.

496
00:35:45,501 --> 00:35:46,800
It had limited range.

497
00:35:47,260 --> 00:35:50,080
It had insufficient lift for any sort of sustained flights.

498
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:53,720
And by and large, it was not a practical airplane.

499
00:35:54,080 --> 00:35:55,800
So the Wrights were excited by their success,

500
00:35:55,940 --> 00:35:59,100
but they knew they had a long road ahead of them and a lot of work to do.

501
00:36:00,320 --> 00:36:02,780
No longer dependent on Kitty Hawk's winds,

502
00:36:03,360 --> 00:36:05,700
the Wrights shifted their entire operation to Dayton.

503
00:36:06,880 --> 00:36:11,120
In secret, they continued their test flying at a remote pasture called Huffman Prairie.

504
00:36:11,121 --> 00:36:19,040
Two years of tinkering and two prototypes later, they produced the world's first practical

505
00:36:19,041 --> 00:36:21,420
airplane, the Wright Flyer 3.

506
00:36:22,420 --> 00:36:26,800
The Wrights made three major improvements on this plane over the 1903 Flyer.

507
00:36:27,040 --> 00:36:32,340
First, the elevators are a full four feet farther forward than their earlier plane,

508
00:36:32,341 --> 00:36:34,780
and the elevators are almost twice as large.

509
00:36:35,360 --> 00:36:37,560
This helped with the stability of the aircraft.

510
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:43,040
They also moved the rudder farther to the rear of the aircraft and increased its size,

511
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:45,500
again aiding the stability of the plane.

512
00:36:45,920 --> 00:36:50,060
The Wrights also separated the controls for wing warping and for rudder control.

513
00:36:50,680 --> 00:36:54,820
And although this gave the pilot more to do, it made the aircraft much more stable,

514
00:36:55,380 --> 00:36:58,340
controllable, and it really helped to make it a practical machine.

515
00:36:59,100 --> 00:37:03,400
In 1907, the Wrights developed a new airplane, the Model A.

516
00:37:04,120 --> 00:37:06,240
The pilot now sat upright at the controls.

517
00:37:07,560 --> 00:37:12,060
To launch it in Dayton's light winds, they constructed a catapult device.

518
00:37:13,060 --> 00:37:16,460
A 1,400-pound weight was dropped from the top of a small tower,

519
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:20,660
pulling a line under the guide rail that propelled the craft forward.

520
00:37:22,900 --> 00:37:26,040
The Wrights could now stay aloft for as long as a half hour,

521
00:37:26,840 --> 00:37:30,160
executing graceful figure eights in total mastery of the sky.

522
00:37:31,800 --> 00:37:34,680
But as Wilbur and Orville tried to secure their patents,

523
00:37:34,880 --> 00:37:36,660
they suddenly stopped flying.

524
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:44,160
Patent laws at that time said that while the patent was being evaluated,

525
00:37:44,420 --> 00:37:48,880
you were not supposed to reveal details of your invention in public.

526
00:37:48,881 --> 00:37:57,560
And there was also the concern that somebody else would steal their idea and make money off of it.

527
00:37:59,480 --> 00:38:01,880
1907 was a dark year for the Wrights.

528
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:07,340
As they faced the daunting task of selling an airplane that they refused to show to any prospective buyer,

529
00:38:07,560 --> 00:38:12,940
The Wright brothers took their design for their plane to the United States Army,

530
00:38:13,200 --> 00:38:18,820
and they could not understand why the Army wouldn't buy this without seeing it perform.

531
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:24,020
They had grown up in this household where you believed a person's word,

532
00:38:24,200 --> 00:38:27,400
and so that's what they expected of other people.

533
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:34,100
As the Wrights ran into brick walls as salesmen, others began stumbling into the air.

534
00:38:34,101 --> 00:38:38,400
In France, renowned balloonist Alberto Santos Dumont

535
00:38:38,401 --> 00:38:42,700
flew 200 feet in an ungainly craft that was unable to turn.

536
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:45,900
It smashed its landing gear when it came down,

537
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:47,180
and it cracked its propellers,

538
00:38:47,320 --> 00:38:49,060
but there was no denying that the man had flown.

539
00:38:49,400 --> 00:38:52,080
And so, as far as anybody in France could understand,

540
00:38:52,480 --> 00:38:55,200
he, Santos, was the man who had truly invented the airplane.

541
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:57,840
This had to be a very frustrating time for the Wright brothers

542
00:38:57,841 --> 00:39:00,300
because they knew they had a superior airplane.

543
00:39:00,301 --> 00:39:03,280
But here, all types of people were getting

544
00:39:03,281 --> 00:39:06,420
all types of recognition for inferior aircraft.

545
00:39:08,080 --> 00:39:10,840
Wilbur and Orville still refused to fly publicly,

546
00:39:11,860 --> 00:39:15,660
insisting to a dubious world that they had long ago conquered the air.

547
00:39:16,320 --> 00:39:18,940
The world was calling them fakers.

548
00:39:19,400 --> 00:39:22,000
The French were calling them bluffeuses,

549
00:39:22,240 --> 00:39:24,300
or as the French meant it, liars.

550
00:39:24,500 --> 00:39:28,580
But there was still that reluctance to do a public demonstration

551
00:39:28,581 --> 00:39:31,760
for fear that somebody would steal the idea.

552
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:40,980
By 1908, five years after Kitty Hawk, the Wrights finally secured their patents and two lucrative contracts.

553
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:47,360
They sold a still unproven flyer to the United States government for $25,000

554
00:39:47,361 --> 00:39:52,180
and sealed a deal with a French syndicate for 500,000 francs.

555
00:39:52,840 --> 00:39:57,760
On August 8th in Le Mans, France, Wilbur prepared to silence the skeptics.

556
00:40:03,280 --> 00:40:07,260
People were probably expecting to see what they had always seen,

557
00:40:07,261 --> 00:40:10,960
an airplane to hop across a field for a few hundred feet

558
00:40:10,961 --> 00:40:14,920
or maybe attempt sort of a squirrely fishtail turn.

559
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:16,860
That's not what happened.

560
00:40:21,660 --> 00:40:24,100
He flies on down along his course,

561
00:40:24,400 --> 00:40:27,480
and then directly in his path is a grove of trees.

562
00:40:28,140 --> 00:40:30,540
And so Wilbur uses his wing warping,

563
00:40:30,800 --> 00:40:32,740
and he banks sharply to the left,

564
00:40:32,741 --> 00:40:36,100
and the spectators in the grandstand, they start to scream.

565
00:40:36,460 --> 00:40:38,820
They think that his plane is out of control, he's going to crash.

566
00:40:39,140 --> 00:40:43,540
But he now uses his rudder and he sweeps through a successful banked turn.

567
00:40:44,700 --> 00:40:47,320
And the screams from the audience turn to cheers.

568
00:40:47,680 --> 00:40:49,820
And they had never ever seen anything like this.

569
00:40:50,060 --> 00:40:54,640
Here was flight with power, agility and precision never even dreamed of.

570
00:40:55,040 --> 00:40:57,580
In 1908, this is a miracle.

571
00:40:58,020 --> 00:40:59,380
This is a miracle.

572
00:40:59,381 --> 00:41:06,220
and you read tales of women fainting, men crying, all this kind of thing,

573
00:41:06,660 --> 00:41:11,760
because they were saying nothing less than a miracle to them at that time, man flying.

574
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:17,180
A month after Wilbur's triumph at Le Mans, it was Orville's turn.

575
00:41:18,620 --> 00:41:21,540
At Fort Myers in Virginia, before awestruck crowds,

576
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:24,720
he demonstrated his flyer for the United States Army.

577
00:41:24,721 --> 00:41:31,720
He wowed government officials with low-level acrobatics and masterful endurance flights,

578
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:34,320
lasting as long as 74 minutes.

579
00:41:35,840 --> 00:41:39,580
The Wrights had recaptured their glory and their place in history.

580
00:41:40,840 --> 00:41:46,500
Fame never changed them, but legal battles with rivals who copied their innovations exhausted Wilbur.

581
00:41:47,960 --> 00:41:53,100
In 1912, he contracted typhoid fever and died at age 45.

582
00:41:54,720 --> 00:42:01,480
Without his brother, Orville lost his passion to advance flight and sold the fledgling Wright Aircraft Company.

583
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:09,400
For the next three decades, he was content to accept the world's accolades and tinker at his home in Dayton.

584
00:42:10,160 --> 00:42:11,700
He died in 1948.

585
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:21,620
Two brothers whose background belied their brilliance shaped the century to come more profoundly than presidents or power brokers.

586
00:42:21,621 --> 00:42:26,620
At Kitty Hawk, Yankee Ingenuity took wing.

587
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:28,420
Some of the brightest and the best,

588
00:42:28,520 --> 00:42:31,520
the most well-financed people have been trying to do it.

589
00:42:31,620 --> 00:42:36,060
But here's two young men who never went to college,

590
00:42:36,160 --> 00:42:40,280
who were self-financed, who operated a bicycle shop,

591
00:42:40,380 --> 00:42:42,580
who in a short period of four years

592
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:45,580
succeeded where everybody else had failed.

593
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:47,580
The Wrights' stories say to us

594
00:42:47,680 --> 00:42:50,180
that the common man can succeed.

595
00:42:50,181 --> 00:42:52,180
It says the underdog can win.

596
00:42:52,181 --> 00:42:57,180
It says that we can do anything that we set our minds to.

597
00:43:01,180 --> 00:43:07,180
It's a story that today still convinces us that anything is possible.

